botlib on strict-alignment CPUs (SP and MP): AAS_ParseBSPEntities() packs
all entity key/value pairs into one hunk buffer, each bsp_epair_t (three
pointers) right after the previous pair's strings. So most epairs start at
an odd address, and the first store into one (epair->next) traps on SPARC:
SIGBUS (FLTACCESS, stx to ...805) in AAS_ParseBSPEntities during "Map
Loading", on every map. Pad the offset to pointer alignment before each
epair, the same way when sizing the buffer and when filling it, so both
stay in step. x86 only gets the (harmless) padding.

Upstream: not submitted yet; no issue or pull request about it at
https://github.com/iortcw/iortcw (checked 2026-11-24).

--- iortcw-438e7d413b5f7277187c35b032eb0ef9093ae778/SP/code/botlib/be_aas_bspq3.c.orig
+++ iortcw-438e7d413b5f7277187c35b032eb0ef9093ae778/SP/code/botlib/be_aas_bspq3.c
@@ -414,6 +414,8 @@
 			if ( !strcmp( token.string, "}" ) ) {
 				break;
 			}
+			// each epair after the previous strings: keep its pointers aligned
+			bufsize = PAD( bufsize, (int) sizeof( void * ) );
 			bufsize += sizeof( bsp_epair_t );
 			if ( token.type != TT_STRING ) {
 				ScriptError( script, "invalid %s", token.string );
@@ -462,6 +464,7 @@
 			if ( !strcmp( token.string, "}" ) ) {
 				break;
 			}
+			buftrav = buffer + PAD( (int) ( buftrav - buffer ), (int) sizeof( void * ) );
 			epair = (bsp_epair_t *) buftrav; buftrav += sizeof( bsp_epair_t );
 			epair->next = ent->epairs;
 			ent->epairs = epair;
